## Building Interface Hierarchies

### Ord Extends Eq
```xiom
pub interface Eq[T] {
  fn equals(self: &T, other: &T) -> Bool;
}

pub interface Ord[T] extends Eq[T] {
  fn compare(self: &T, other: &T) -> Ordering;
}
```

If a type implements Ord, it MUST also implement Eq. The compiler enforces this.

### Why Inheritance?
```xiom
fn find_duplicates[T: Ord](items: &Vec[T]) -> Bool {
  for i in 0..items.len() {
    for j in i+1..items.len() {
      if items[i] == items[j] { return true; }  // Uses Eq (inherited via Ord)
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

T: Ord implies T: Eq — no need to write both.

### Multi-Level Inheritance
```xiom
pub interface Hash[T] extends Eq[T] {
  fn hash(self: &T) -> Int;
}

pub interface OrdHash[T] extends Ord[T] + Hash[T] {
  // Requires BOTH Ord AND Hash (and by extension, Eq)
}
```

### The Inheritance Diamond
```xiom
pub interface A { fn a(); }
pub interface B extends A { fn b(); }
pub interface C extends A { fn c(); }
pub interface D extends B + C { fn d(); }
// D requires A (once), B, C, and D
// No diamond problem — interfaces don't carry data
```

